Both Large Language Models (LLMs) and Memory Mapping are core concepts in computer science, but they operate at entirely different levels of abstraction. When we talk about "LLM memory," we usually mean how the model retains information from your conversation. When we talk about "memory mapping" in computing, we are talking about how a computer's operating system manages physical and virtual RAM.

However, the two concepts do intersect when engineers try to run huge LLMs efficiently.

Here is a detailed breakdown of both concepts, how they interact, and an arrow diagram of memory mapping.

Part 1: LLM "Memory" vs. Computing "Memory Mapping"
1. Large Language Model (LLM) Memory
When an LLM (like GPT-4 or Llama) is running, it does not have long-term memory like a human database. It is stateless. This means if you ask it a question, then ask another, it won't naturally remember the first question.

Developers use three main tricks to give the illusion of memory:

Context Window (Working Memory): The LLM receives the entire previous conversation as part of your new prompt. If you have had 10 turns, all 10 turns are summarized or sent verbatim along with turn 11. This acts as the LLM's "scratchpad." Its limit is called the Context Window (e.g., 32k tokens).

Model Parameters (Trained Knowledge): This is the model’s "hard drive." Everything it learned during training is stored here as weights (numbers). This memory is frozen unless the model is fine-tuned.

External Memory (RAG): If the model needs to remember something specific (like your customer file) outside its window, developers use Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) to lookup that data from a database and paste it into the context window before sending the prompt.

2. Computing Memory Mapping (mmap)
This is a operating system (OS) technique, nothing to do with AI specifically.

Memory mapping allows an application to treat a file on the hard drive as if it were already loaded into the computer’s RAM. Instead of explicitly calling "Read File" and "Write File," the program just reads and writes to a memory address. The OS handles the lazy loading of data in and out of actual RAM on demand.

Why this is crucial for running LLMs:
LLM weights (the trained knowledge) are massive. A Llama-3-70B model requires roughly 70GB of memory to load. Most computers don't have 70GB of free RAM or GPU memory (VRAM).

If an engineer uses memory mapping for the model file:

The OS maps the entire 70GB file into the application’s Virtual Address Space (a logical map).

It does not load 70GB into RAM immediately.

When the model needs a specific set of weights to generate a word, the OS detects a "page fault," fetches those specific bytes from the hard drive, pops them into RAM momentarily, and uses them.

Part 2: How to Open an LLM and Check Memory Mapping
You cannot "open" an LLM like a PDF. An LLM runs as a process (a program in execution). To check its memory mapping, you must use OS tools to look at how that specific process is using RAM.

Assuming you are running a local LLM using a tool like llama.cpp or Ollama.

A. On Linux
The best way to see memory mapping is using the /proc filesystem.

Find the Process ID (PID):

Bash
ps aux | grep llama
# Let's assume the PID is 12345
View the Maps: This shows every single memory region allocated to the process, what file it is mapped to, and its permissions (Read, Write, Execute).

Bash
cat /proc/12345/maps
View Human-Readable Summary (PmapSize):

Bash
pmap -x 12345
B. On Windows
Use Process Explorer (a free official Microsoft tool).

Open Process Explorer.

Find your LLM process (e.g., ollama.exe).

Right-click it and select Properties.

Go to the Memory tab.

Virtual Size: Total memory mapped (can be huge).

Resident Set Size (RSS): Actual RAM being used currently.

Part 3: Arrow Diagram on Memory Mapping
This diagram illustrates how a computing system maps logical addresses to physical hardware. This is generic but is how an LLM's weights are managed if the software uses memory mapping for a large model file.

Code snippet
graph TD
    %% Define Nodes
    subgraph CPU_Execution
        direction TB
        Instruction[CPU Instruction needs data at Virtual Address: 0x4005A0]
        MMU[Memory Management Unit (Hardware)]
    end

    subgraph OS_Management
        direction TB
        PageTable[Page Table\n(Maps Virtual Page # to Physical Frame #)]
        OS[Operating System kernel\n(Handles Page Faults)]
    end

    subgraph Physical_Hardware
        direction TB
        RAM[Physical RAM\n(Divided into Frames)]
        Storage[Storage Disk / Swap\n(e.g., Llama Model File)]
    end

    %% Define Flow Arrows
    Instruction -->|Req: 0x4005A0| MMU
    MMU -.->|Lookup| PageTable

    %% Path 1: Page Hit (It is already in RAM)
    PageTable == "|Valid Bit: 1| -> Frame 12" ===> RAM
    MMU -->|Translate| RAM
    RAM -->|Return Data| Instruction

    %% Path 2: Page Fault (It is NOT in RAM)
    PageTable -.-x|Valid Bit: 0| OS
    OS -->|1. Detect Page Fault| OS
    OS -->|2. Find Free Frame in RAM| RAM
    OS -->|3. Read Data from Disk| Storage
    Storage -->|4. Load Data| RAM
    OS -->|5. Update Page Table| PageTable
    OS -.->|6. Retry Instruction| Instruction

    %% Styles for clarity
    classDef hardware fill:#f9f,stroke:#333,stroke-width:2px;
    classDef software fill:#bbf,stroke:#333,stroke-width:1px;
    classDef fault fill:#faa,stroke:#f00,stroke-width:2px;
    class MMU,RAM,Storage hardware;
    class PageTable,OS,Instruction software;
    class OS,Storage fault;
Diagram Legend:
Virtual Address: The logical address the running LLM program thinks it is accessing (e.g., 0x4005A0).

MMU (Memory Management Unit): The hardware in the CPU that translates virtual addresses to physical addresses very quickly.

Page Table: A table managed by the OS that stores the translation rules: "Virtual Page 10 is at Physical Frame 500."

Page Hit (Green Flow): The data is already in RAM. The MMU looks up the translation, finds it, and gets the data immediately.

Page Fault (Red Flow): The data is mapped, but not loaded. The MMU fails to find the translation and triggers a CPU exception, passing control to the OS. The OS must fetch the data from the slow storage disk and put it in RAM.